{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 3, :comments ["<b>Or swear an oath to impose a prohibition.</b> What is the difference between a vow and an oath? A vow — one vows by the life of the King; an oath — one swears by the King Himself. And even though there is no proof for this, there is an allusion: ״חי HaShem וחי נפשך אם אעזבך״ (as HaShem lives and as your soul lives, if I leave you)." "<b>He shall not profane his word.</b> Like ״יחל ישראל אל HaShem״ — meaning, his word shall not be softened, but he shall do according to all that proceeds from his mouth. Another interpretation: <b>he shall not profane his word</b> — he shall not profane his word to himself, meaning, he may not permit his own vow to himself, but others may permit it for him. [לא יחל — לא יתבטל, i.e. he shall not annul]."], :book :numbers, :voice :chizkuni, :lang :en, :chapter 30, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T15:20:15.751639858Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}